<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="need-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="need-title">Do you need to register?</h2>
      <p>This is a starting guide, not a ruling on your business. Limits have exceptions and can change.</p>
    </header>
    <ol class="decide">
      <li>
        <h3>Do you sell goods to another state, or through an e-commerce platform that collects tax?</h3>
        <p><strong>If yes,</strong> you may need to register regardless of turnover. Section 24 of the CGST Act lists categories such as those making inter-State taxable supplies. Notifications relax this for some, reportedly including smaller suppliers of services, so check before your first sale.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Are you required to pay tax for some other reason, such as under reverse charge?</h3>
        <p><strong>If yes,</strong> section 24 applies to persons required to pay tax under reverse charge. Ask a professional.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>If not, what is your turnover across the year?</h3>
        <p>Section 22 sets <strong>₹20 lakh</strong> outside the special category States. A separate notification is reported to raise this to <strong>₹40 lakh</strong> for those supplying only goods within a state, subject to conditions. Mixed businesses should ask how the limit applies.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Under the limit? Consider whether to register anyway.</h3>
        <p>See below. Registering by choice brings duties that you must then keep up.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="choice-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="choice-title">Registering by choice: both sides</h2>
      <dl class="fit-list">
        <div><dt>May help when</dt><dd>Your customers are registered businesses that want a GST invoice, you want to claim input tax credit, or a platform or buyer requires it.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>May not help when</dt><dd>You sell to consumers who cannot use the credit, as prices may effectively rise.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>The commitment</dt><dd>Returns are due even in months with no sales, and missing them has consequences.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>What the process generally needs</h2>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>PAN of the business or proprietor</li>
        <li>Aadhaar authentication of the responsible person</li>
        <li>Proof of the principal place of business</li>
        <li>Bank account details</li>
        <li>Entity documents, for companies, LLPs and firms</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="para">Follow the current list on the {% ext "https://www.gst.gov.in/", "GST portal" %}, which is where you apply. Expect questions from the officer if the address proof is unclear.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="help-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="help-title" class="first-h2">If you pay someone to help</h2>
      <p>Paid help can be useful for choosing the right classification, preparing documents and answering officer queries. Before paying, get clear on these:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Split the fee.</strong> Ask what goes to any government head and what is the professional's charge. Ask them to show you on the portal if a government fee is claimed.</li>
        <li><strong>No one can promise approval.</strong> An officer may ask questions or reject an application. Be wary of anyone who says approval is certain.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep it in your name.</strong> The registered mobile number and email should be yours, because they control the account.</li>
        <li><strong>Ask what comes next.</strong> Who files the first returns? Registration starts a recurring duty.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="fast">The faster route you may read about</h2>
      <p>Secondary sources describe a simplified route under Rule 14A from 1 November 2025: quicker approval for smaller taxpayers below a reported monthly output tax limit, with Aadhaar authentication. We have not read the notification itself, so check its conditions on the GST portal before choosing this route.</p>

      <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Assuming the turnover limit always applies,</strong> when a section 24 case applies from the first sale.</li>
        <li><strong>Using an address you cannot prove,</strong> or a rented place without suitable documents.</li>
        <li><strong>Choosing the wrong goods or service codes,</strong> which leads to questions later.</li>
        <li><strong>Registering and then forgetting returns,</strong> including nil returns where required.</li>
        <li><strong>Picking the Composition Scheme without checking its limits.</strong> It restricts some activities, so ask.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="after">After you register</h2>
      <p>Show your GSTIN where required, raise proper tax invoices, keep records and start filing. {% routeLink "/services/gst-return-filing/", "GST return filing" %} explains what that involves. Some businesses also want {% routeLink "/services/msme-udyam-registration/", "Udyam registration" %}, which is a separate thing and is free on its official portal.</p>

